{
  "success": true,
  "output": "I've done a thorough pass over the data layer (`lib/db`, `lib/server`), the schema, every API route, all 20 client components, and the page tree. Overall finding up front: **this codebase is unusually disciplined already** — the server layer has explicit comments about avoiding N+1s, batches counts with `Promise.all`, and there are zero query-in-a-loop patterns anywhere (`grep` for `.map(async` / loop-awaited queries came back empty). So most of the classic complaints don't apply here. What follows are the real, verified gaps.\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns — none found, but a real scaling risk hides in the schema\n\nNo loop fires a query per row anywhere in `lib/server/*`. `getQueuePage` (lib/server/conflicts.ts:172) is a textbook example of doing it right: rows, counts, and total run as one `Promise.all`, then a single grouped `countChildrenByParent` call covers the whole page instead of one query per row.\n\nThe actual risk is **missing indexes on foreign-key/filter columns**. Postgres only auto-indexes primary keys, not foreign keys — and almost every hot query filters or joins on an unindexed FK:\n\n| Table.column | Used by | \n|---|---|\n| `conflicts.runId` | every queue query, `getQueueCounts`, `getSeverityBreakdown`, export — the entry-point filter for nearly the whole app |\n| `audit_log.runId` | `listRunAudit`, `getAuditPage` — on a table defined as **append-only, never shrinks** |\n| `resolutions.conflictId` | left-joined in every queue/export read |\n| `matches.entityAId/entityBId` | joined per conflict row |\n| `entities.parentExternalId` | `countChildrenByParent`, called on every queue page load |\n\n`entities` has one composite index (`entities_run_source_external_idx` on `runId, sourceId, externalId`), but it doesn't cover `parentExternalId`, which is what `countChildrenByParent` actually filters/groups on (lib/server/entities.ts:28). Today this is invisible because tables are small; it degrades to sequential scans as runs accumulate — `audit_log` especially, since it's designed to only grow.\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId)]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [index(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId)]);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId)]);\n\nexport const entities = pgTable(\"entities\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"entities_run_source_external_idx\").on(table.runId, table.sourceId, table.externalId),\n  index(\"entities_run_parent_idx\").on(table.runId, table.parentExternalId), // add this\n]);\n```\nThen `npm run db:generate` to produce the migration. The `resolutions.conflictId` index is also worth making `.unique()` — the code comment at lib/server/conflicts.ts:64 already flags that a duplicate resolution row would silently double-count the queue.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\nReal one: none of the row components in the conflict queue are memoized, and the parent recreates a closure per row on every render — for a screen the code itself says is built for \"a reviewer working a queue of 400\" (components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:19-33).\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx — ledger view\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}                 // new fn every render\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}                        // new fn every render\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)} // new fn every render\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n`ConflictDiffRow`, `ConflictDenseTable`'s row, and `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow` aren't wrapped in `React.memo`. Every `j`/`k` keystroke changes `selectedIndex` in the parent, which re-renders `ConflictQueue`, which re-creates all ~100-400 row callbacks and re-renders every row — even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected rows visually change.\n\nFix: memoize the row and pass stable identifiers instead of closures, so the child invokes the shared callback itself:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  row, index, selected, onSelect, onSubmit, ...\n}: { row: QueueRow; index: number; onSelect: (index: number) => void; onSubmit: ReviewSubmit; ... }) {\n  return <article onMouseDown={() => onSelect(index)} ...>\n    <ReviewActions submit={onSubmit} .../>\n  </article>;\n});\n\n// conflict-queue.tsx\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\n// pass `submit` (already stable via useCallback in useReview) and `row.conflictId` down;\n// have the row call submit(row.conflictId, action) itself instead of receiving a\n// pre-bound closure.\n```\nWith `React.memo` + stable callback identities, a keystroke re-renders 2 rows instead of the whole page. Same pattern applies to `conflict-dense-table.tsx` and `conflict-focus.tsx`'s `ListRow`.\n\nEverything else checked out clean — `Elapsed` and the keydown listener use proper dependency arrays, `submitFor`/`move`/`setOverrideOpen` are already correctly memoized with `useCallback`, and `NewRunForm`'s filtered candidate lists are correctly wrapped in `useMemo`.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\nThe app deliberately has almost no client-side fetching (AGENTS.md-driven: everything is a Server Component reading Postgres directly, TanStack Query is scoped to *just* run-status polling in `query-provider.tsx`). That's a good call and I'm not fighting it.\n\nWhat's missing is **request-independent caching for slow-changing reference data**. `listSources()`, `listRulesets()`, and `getActiveRuleset()` hit Postgres fresh on every page load (`/rules`, `/runs/new`, `/sources`), even though sources and rulesets change rarely (a ruleset is only written via the explicit \"save as vN\" flow). Unlike `fetch()`, Drizzle queries aren't deduped or cached by Next's Data Cache automatically.\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache, revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources:list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n\nexport async function createSource(input: CreateSource): Promise<Source> {\n  const [row] = await getDb().insert(sources).values(input).returning();\n  revalidateTag(\"sources\"); // invalidate immediately on write, not on a timer\n  return sourceSchema.parse(row);\n}\n```\nSame pattern for `listRulesets`/`getActiveRuleset`, tagged `\"rulesets\"` and invalidated in `createRulesetVersion`. This removes a DB round trip from three navigation paths with zero staleness risk, since invalidation is explicit on write rather than time-based.\n\nNote: `staleTime: 0` on the run-status query client (components/query-provider.tsx:26) looks like a missed caching opportunity but isn't — it's polling live engine state, and the comment there correctly explains why caching would be wrong for that one case.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\nNone found. Every subscription has a matching cleanup:\n- `conflict-queue.tsx:130-131` — `addEventListener(\"keydown\", ...)` is removed in the effect's return.\n- `run-live.tsx:106-107` — `setInterval` is cleared in the effect's return, and only runs at all `if (running)`.\n- `conflict-focus.tsx`'s `ListRow` scroll effect has a correct, narrow dependency array (`[selected]`), so it doesn't re-subscribe on unrelated re-renders.\n\nWorth calling out as validated rather than silently skipped — this is the category where a hastily-written app usually leaks the most, and this one doesn't.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\nOnly one genuine, minor one. `getExportSizing` (lib/server/export.ts:311) runs two separate `COUNT(*)` queries where the codebase's own established pattern (`getSeverityBreakdown`, `getQueueCounts`) would do it in one grouped query:\n\n```ts\n// Before — 2 round trips\nconst [hierarchy, decisions, orphans] = await Promise.all([\n  countAccounts(run.id, run.sourceAId),\n  countConflicts(run.id),\n  countConflicts(run.id, \"orphan\"),\n]);\n\n// After — 1 round trip, same shape as getSeverityBreakdown\nconst [hierarchy, byClass] = await Promise.all([\n  countAccounts(run.id, run.sourceAId),\n  getDb()\n    .select({ conflictClass: conflicts.conflictClass, total: count() })\n    .from(conflicts)\n    .where(eq(conflicts.runId, run.id))\n    .groupBy(conflicts.conflictClass),\n]);\nconst decisions = byClass.reduce((sum, r) => sum + r.total, 0);\nconst orphans = byClass.find((r) => r.conflictClass === \"orphan\")?.total ?? 0;\n```\nLow impact (one extra `COUNT` query on the export-preview screen only), but worth fixing for consistency since it's the exact pattern the codebase already uses elsewhere.\n\n---\n\n**Priority order if you're picking one thing to do first:** add the missing indexes (#1) — that's the one that gets worse over time and is invisible until a run/audit table is large. The re-render fix (#2) matters most once someone actually loads a 400-row escalation queue, which per the code's own comments is the explicit design target.\n",
